{"type": "messages", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the isolated science-reviewer worker in Research World. Obey the supplied evidence and role boundary. Return one JSON object and no prose."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Review only scientific validity and inference scope. Return {\"findings\":[{\"check_id\":\"CITE-CONTEXT|SCOPE-INFLATE|ARGUMENT-CHAIN-BREAK|RESULT-ARTIFACT-MISMATCH\",\"severity\":\"info|minor|major|critical\",\"evidence\":[{\"id\":str,\"locator\":str,\"sha256\":str}],\"recommendation\":str}],\"summary\":str}. Use major only when the direction result cannot be admitted without repair.\nINPUT:\n{\"plan\": {\"claim_index\": 2, \"setup\": [\"Define deterministic mass-action transmission function assuming homogeneous host mixing\", \"Generate synthetic historical contact-rate and spillover-event pairs representing past outbreaks\", \"Compute predicted versus observed values and quantify directional bias across contact heterogeneity\"], \"command\": [\"python3 mass_action_calibration.py\"], \"files\": {\"mass_action_calibration.py\": \"import json\\n\\ndef main():\\n    N_reservoir = 1000\\n    N_human = 500\\n    beta = 0.001\\n    obs_contacts = [0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0]\\n    obs_events = [0, 1, 3, 8, 20]\\n    preds = []\\n    for c in obs_contacts:\\n        pred = (N_reservoir * N_human * c * beta) / (N_reservoir + N_human)\\n        preds.append(round(pred, 2))\\n    biases = [round(p - o, 2) for p, o in zip(preds, obs_events)]\\n    mean_bias = round(sum(biases) / len(biases), 2)\\n    result = {\\n        'metrics': {'predictions': preds, 'observations': obs_events, 'biases': biases, 'mean_bias': mean_bias},\\n        'interpretation': 'The deterministic mass-action baseline systematically overestimates spillover at low contact rates and underestimates at high rates, confirming that homogeneous mixing assumptions fail to capture episodic, heterogeneous wildlife-livestock-human interfaces.',\\n        'limitations': 'Cannot establish real-world diagnostic sensitivity, reporting lags, or multi-pathway transmission networks; purely theoretical calibration without empirical field data.'\\n    }\\n    print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\\n\\nif __name__ == '__main__':\\n    main()\"}, \"seed\": 42, \"expected_observation\": \"JSON output containing predictions, observations, biases, and mean_bias showing systematic over-prediction at low contacts and under-prediction at high contacts, with interpretation noting homogeneous mixing failure.\", \"cannot_establish\": \"Real-world surveillance gaps, stochastic spillover thresholds, and dynamic ecological monitoring data required for operational early-warning deployment.\"}, \"execution\": {\"id\": \"execution:2e8aff18c81263f636174342\", \"project_id\": \"project:b6302a8f113bb4371073ef1fa21298690aa2fc5e3d255f1933410c94f0831fa1\", \"attempt_id\": \"attempt:9420c126411eb1fe9b054395\", \"environment_id\": \"environment:5ccc9ae7d8a7fc5f4b57453c6f32bd5cd2acced9c382c0fd58824c8a2ca9c646\", \"image_digest\": \"sha256:88c0df41fded2ce82c84dcfe4ba86f62359a86a05165cc81d8f069ddd9359e38\", \"command\": [\"python\", \"mass_action_calibration.py\"], \"input_artifact_id\": \"artifact:976992fdc2b91893f38ed2f1884d32342653ddf94c13eb2b677c732b08d7c36b\", \"input_hash\": \"976992fdc2b91893f38ed2f1884d32342653ddf94c13eb2b677c732b08d7c36b\", \"seed\": 42, \"spec\": {\"image\": \"sha256:88c0df41fded2ce82c84dcfe4ba86f62359a86a05165cc81d8f069ddd9359e38\", \"command\": [\"python\", \"mass_action_calibration.py\"], \"input_artifact_id\": \"artifact:976992fdc2b91893f38ed2f1884d32342653ddf94c13eb2b677c732b08d7c36b\", \"seed\": 42, \"files\": {\"mass_action_calibration.py\": \"\"}, \"network\": \"none\", \"read_only\": true, \"limits\": {\"cpus\": 1, \"memory_mb\": 512, \"pids\": 128}}, \"exit_code\": 0, \"output_artifact_id\": \"artifact:97019617085357e1375e7767411a1f42e9d5d338d6add7c27e2f3fb7ef54f586\", \"output_hash\": \"97019617085357e1375e7767411a1f42e9d5d338d6add7c27e2f3fb7ef54f586\", \"usage\": {\"wall_ms\": 391, \"replay_verified\": true, \"replay_output_hash\": \"97019617085357e1375e7767411a1f42e9d5d338d6add7c27e2f3fb7ef54f586\"}, \"created_at\": \"2026-08-13T19:07:01.883303+00:00\", \"stdout\": \"{\\n  \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n    \\\"predictions\\\": [\\n      0.03,\\n      0.17,\\n      0.33,\\n      0.67,\\n      1.67\\n    ],\\n    \\\"observations\\\": [\\n      0,\\n      1,\\n      3,\\n      8,\\n      20\\n    ],\\n    \\\"biases\\\": [\\n      0.03,\\n      -0.83,\\n      -2.67,\\n      -7.33,\\n      -18.33\\n    ],\\n    \\\"mean_bias\\\": -5.83\\n  },\\n  \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"The deterministic mass-action baseline systematically overestimates spillover at low contact rates and underestimates at high rates, confirming that homogeneous mixing assumptions fail to capture episodic, heterogeneous wildlife-livestock-human interfaces.\\\",\\n  \\\"limitations\\\": \\\"Cannot establish real-world diagnostic sensitivity, reporting lags, or multi-pathway transmission networks; purely theoretical calibration without empirical field data.\\\"\\n}\\n\"}, \"claims\": {\"claims\": [{\"node_id\": \"node:0de17ce2d167baa96306b214288ba47f\", \"text\": \"High-risk SARS-like coronavirus spillover clusters are predominantly situated within 11–20% proximity to healthcare facilities, whereas areas >1% distant exhibit marked healthcare access inequalities, particularly when livestock secondary hosts are absent.\", \"kind\": \"evidence\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:5f88fef84cf0a17681d3836788727eb730f625cdc64a8dd65cf92f735b733b7e\"], \"scope\": \"Spatial risk clustering and healthcare access inequality analysis for SARS-like coronaviruses across identified high-risk clusters.\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 0, \"challenge\": \"The '11–20% proximity' metric is geographically ambiguous and conflates physical closeness with functional healthcare accessibility, ignoring socioeconomic barriers, infrastructure deficits, and actual diagnostic capacity that determine whether proximity translates to detectable surveillance.\", \"repair\": \"Replace percentage-based distance proxies with standardized travel-time or service-area metrics, and integrate health-system readiness indices to differentiate mere geographic adjacency from actionable clinical detection potential.\"}}, {\"node_id\": \"node:9d706cc2dd5c065630436a792b597fe7\", \"text\": \"Ecological niche modeling under IPCC 4th Assessment climate scenarios predicts a measurable increase in environmental suitability for human monkeypox transmission in eastern Democratic Republic of Congo by 2050 and 2080, driven by forest clearing and climatic variables.\", \"kind\": \"prediction\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:b0631a85c9c59ae935be22c2f25b9deb6c0e181d45b747636c7b8ceb133b7f73\"], \"scope\": \"Contemporary and projected climate-driven habitat suitability modeling for MPXV reservoirs and human cases in Central Africa.\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 1, \"challenge\": \"Reliance on outdated IPCC Fourth Assessment scenarios and static ecological niche modeling neglects dynamic land-use governance, vector adaptation, and non-climatic drivers like wildlife trade, risking spatially misaligned or temporally obsolete range-shift projections.\", \"repair\": \"Update projections using IPCC AR6 climate ensembles coupled with dynamic land-cover change models, and incorporate dispersal constraints and anthropogenic mobility data to refine spatiotemporal risk forecasts.\"}}, {\"node_id\": \"node:69f8fb93712bd90000fce953f978b2ae\", \"text\": \"Mathematical baselines for cross-species transmission probability can be derived from mass-action contact rate quantifications among reservoir hosts, intermediate species, and humans, though empirical calibration against historical outbreak datasets remains an unresolved prerequisite for operational early-warning deployment.\", \"kind\": \"bound\", \"source_ids\": [\"source-snapshot:af7ea0b8796da83a6a572334fe3402063aa06d9f7ea2fbc468bddbf20e5d7fa4\"], \"scope\": \"Foundational mathematical modeling and principle-based risk prediction for viral spillover, excluding empirical validation.\", \"rebuttal\": {\"claim_index\": 2, \"challenge\": \"The mass-action framework assumes homogeneous host mixing and constant contact rates, which contradicts the heterogeneous, episodic nature of wildlife-livestock-human interfaces and ignores stochastic spillover dynamics essential for reliable early-warning deployment.\", \"repair\": \"Augment the deterministic mass-action baseline with stochastic branching-process models and integrate real-time ecological monitoring data to capture contact heterogeneity and establish empirically validated calibration thresholds.\"}}], \"rebuttal\": {\"rebuttals\": [{\"claim_index\": 0, \"challenge\": \"The '11–20% proximity' metric is geographically ambiguous and conflates physical closeness with functional healthcare accessibility, ignoring socioeconomic barriers, infrastructure deficits, and actual diagnostic capacity that determine whether proximity translates to detectable surveillance.\", \"repair\": \"Replace percentage-based distance proxies with standardized travel-time or service-area metrics, and integrate health-system readiness indices to differentiate mere geographic adjacency from actionable clinical detection potential.\"}, {\"claim_index\": 1, \"challenge\": \"Reliance on outdated IPCC Fourth Assessment scenarios and static ecological niche modeling neglects dynamic land-use governance, vector adaptation, and non-climatic drivers like wildlife trade, risking spatially misaligned or temporally obsolete range-shift projections.\", \"repair\": \"Update projections using IPCC AR6 climate ensembles coupled with dynamic land-cover change models, and incorporate dispersal constraints and anthropogenic mobility data to refine spatiotemporal risk forecasts.\"}, {\"claim_index\": 2, \"challenge\": \"The mass-action framework assumes homogeneous host mixing and constant contact rates, which contradicts the heterogeneous, episodic nature of wildlife-livestock-human interfaces and ignores stochastic spillover dynamics essential for reliable early-warning deployment.\", \"repair\": \"Augment the deterministic mass-action baseline with stochastic branching-process models and integrate real-time ecological monitoring data to capture contact heterogeneity and establish empirically validated calibration thresholds.\"}]}, \"review\": {\"findings\": [{\"check_id\": \"CITE-CONTEXT\", \"severity\": \"minor\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:5f88fef84cf0a17681d3836788727eb730f625cdc64a8dd65cf92f735b733b7e\", \"locator\": \"1-441\", \"sha256\": \"4ad4b0c0e0e31a3d7dbe71e007f6e220e5faf3845538396c070c4fb5b4436e43\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:b0631a85c9c59ae935be22c2f25b9deb6c0e181d45b747636c7b8ceb133b7f73\", \"locator\": \"1-575\", \"sha256\": \"9eff09096eee55b47f7284f4d77adec229bfe23327871d77f12d3d7fc6fb4c1d\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Explicitly include Zika virus literature and clarify the specific backtesting methodology to ensure citation context tightly matches the stated directional scope.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"SCOPE-INFLATE\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapshot:af7ea0b8796da83a6a572334fe3402063aa06d9f7ea2fbc468bddbf20e5d7fa4\", \"locator\": \"1-552\", \"sha256\": \"b5ec489dff824fbbc9c19bb2f51d13ad66e0d96fadd2957776ec216722673e47\"}, {\"id\": \"source-snapshot:8b4e5499d95f85011a389067008bca829c21ff59219361fcb568cb03f1e08bb4\", \"locator\": \"1-619\", \"sha256\": \"4d1b6eea47841f7d38ca4bd0dfda59a8c4f944cf57e6aabd738f68bd424ea451\"}], \"recommendation\": \"Refrain from claiming 'rigorous backtesting' and 'calibrated probability scores' until empirical validation is performed; restrict scope to the descriptive, theoretical, and historical trigger analyses currently supported.\"}, {\"check_id\": \"ARGUMENT-CHAIN-BREAK\", \"severity\": \"major\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"source-snapsh